This instance illustrates why requesting another demo after a rejected guess is not itself a recovery strategy. The test grid was made of alternating color bands with several short/partial bands; an unsupported first submission homogenized every row. The demos were not of that format: the first showed a large patterned block and separate repeated stripe patterns, while the second showed a patterned field with sparse edge cells. Each demo changed only particular cells to color 1, but their structures and deltas needed independent parsing before transferring any rule to the test.

Reusable checkpoint: after a rejection, record the changed cells and untouched near-matches in every available pair, distinguish potentially independent transformations, and test candidate selectors against all examples before spending another point. A request is justified only by a specific ambiguity that can affect the test prediction. Do not assume that the new pair resembles the test just because it belongs to the same task stream.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545205/attempts/52b150a3511a4455a56f31d31c01d91b/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-evidence-loop/references/session-task-6425b349-audit.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-evidence-loop", "file": "references/session-task-6425b349-review.md", "content": "# Review: heterogeneous demos and rejection recovery

This task's three demos had very different layouts and palettes. Pair 1 changed just three cells in color-3 rows to 1: one isolated 2 at the far edge, then two suffix runs of 2s. Pair 2 changed suffixes of 5s to 1 after 7-runs in two rows. Pair 3 changed a few 2s to 1 in rows containing 5-patterns. Thus the stable clue was not a single shared local row motif; these examples suggest selecting cells by some broader cross-row/column pattern or correspondence, but do not specify it. A proposed output that recolored every 3 in the test was rejected.

Recovery lesson: after rejection, explicitly falsify the submitted selector. The evidence does not warrant broadening that selector from selected color-3 cells to all color-3 cells. Recompute exact deltas per demo and search for the shared global property, including aligned positions, row/column patterns, and unchanged candidates. Distinguish a selected region's footprint from the criterion selecting it. Do not convert a speculative verbal resemblance into confidence, and do not claim that a newly requested demo resolves ambiguity unless its deltas are actually analyzed.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545205/attempts/52b150a3511a4455a56f31d31c01d91b/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-evidence-loop/references/session-task-6425b349-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-turn-discipline", "file": "references/session-review.md", "content": "# Session review: speculative submissions and recovery

A test grid was submitted twice after one demo without first documenting a complete transformation rule. The first answer altered the repeated leftmost marker and separator rows; the next answer changed marker behavior but preserved the rest. Both were rejected. A later attempt restored some input marker cells and then again failed. The useful lesson is not a hidden solution: the demo's broad visual pattern did not uniquely determine how every test marker should be treated, and near-duplicate guesses were costly.

For future instances, compare the actual input/output grids cell by cell immediately after a demo. Explicitly list deltas and untouched lookalikes, then distinguish object selection, geometric effect, and color mapping. After rejection, record and retire the complete prediction rather than trying a minimally changed grid. Another demo is justified only if named competing rules predict different outcomes; otherwise continue analyzing the existing pair. This session did not establish a validated task transformation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545205/attempts/52b150a3511a4455a56f31d31c01d91b/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-turn-discipline/references/session-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-rejection-ledger", "file": "references/color-mask-recovery.md", "content": "# Avoiding unsupported cellwise color masks

This session's input had three vertical zones separated by a solid marker column. The demo mapped a 27×21 grid to a 27×10 result, preserving a selected compact region; output colors encoded a summary rather than a direct recoloring of every test input cell. Two rejected predictions instead produced an 18×11 direct color transformation of the left-side values. The second attempt changed which source color was treated as selected without validating that mapping against the demonstrated output.

For future separator/summary puzzles, explicitly map demo coordinates and region boundaries to output coordinates and compare all rows before predicting. Output width and separator position are clues, not proof of a simple crop. Do not transfer a direct cellwise mask unless it exactly reproduces the demo. After rejection, revisit the representation and mapping rather than changing only a color-selection assumption.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545205/attempts/52b150a3511a4455a56f31d31c01d91b/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-rejection-ledger/references/color-mask-recovery.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-wallpaper-analysis", "file": "references/task-a550626c-review.md", "content": "# Session review: periodic-wallpaper overfit and budget failure

This instance had eight demos and a diagonal-rich 28x28 test. Several demos did produce dense periodic outputs, but they were structurally varied: diagonal seed patterns generated wallpapers with different motifs/periods; other examples were horizontal bands with zeros filled by repeating colors, and one was a checkerboard. The test was repeatedly guessed as a small diagonal modulo-cycle from the visible colors (axes, phases, periods, and palette orders were varied), with seven rejected outputs. Those rejections show that motif guessing was not grounded; the test's exact generating abstraction was never identified.

Lesson: extract exact input/output deltas and derive each demo's output formula before transferring a wallpaper rule. For each candidate, write the explicit `(r,c)->value` mapping and verify every cell across all demos. Do not treat shared dense/repetitive appearance as evidence of shared period, axis, motif, or that output is a full fill. After one rejection, retire the whole rule and reassess structural selection; do not spend remaining attempts on cosmetic phase/order/axis variants. The final attempt should be a distinct, evidence-supported abstraction, not a last-minute guessed cycle.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545205/attempts/52b150a3511a4455a56f31d31c01d91b/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-wallpaper-analysis/references/task-a550626c-review.md"}
